Q3 – Engineer the hashCode–equals Contract
Bloom's Taxonomy: K4 – Analyse
Problem Context: College Fest – Event Attendance De-duplication
Marks: 6

1. Problem analysis
A college fest uses student ID cards to record attendance at multiple entry gates. The same student may be scanned several times.

The application uses HashSet<Student> to store unique students.

The important requirement is that two Student objects with the same registration number must be treated as equal, even if they are different objects.

The junior developer has overridden equals() but has not overridden hashCode().

This violates the Java contract between equals() and hashCode().

The hashCode–equals contract
Java requires:

If two objects are equal according to equals(), they must have the same hashCode().

If two objects have the same hash code, they do not necessarily have to be equal.

The hash code must remain consistent while the information used in its calculation remains unchanged.

For attendance de-duplication, regNo should determine equality.

